„­bible_version£KJV¤book§chapter3¨sections›…¤book§chapter3«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙšThus saith the LORD; Behold, I will raise up against Babylon, and against them that dwell in the midst of them that rise up against me, a destroying wind;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ’and will send unto Babylon fanners, that shall fan her, and shall empty her land: for in the day of trouble they shall be against her round about.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ±Against him that bendeth let the archer bend his bow, and against him that lifteth himself up in his brigandine: and spare ye not her young men; destroy ye utterly all her host.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙhThus the slain shall fall in the land of the Chaldeans, and they that are thrust through in her streets.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ”For Israel hath not been forsaken, nor Judah of his God, of the LORD of hosts; though their land was filled with sin against the Holy One of Israel.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter3«first_verseªlast_verse
¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙµFlee out of the midst of Babylon, and deliver every man his soul: be not cut off in her iniquity; for this is the time of the LORD's vengeance; he will render unto her a recompence.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ˜Babylon hath been a golden cup in the LORD's hand, that made all the earth drunken: the nations have drunken of her wine; therefore the nations are mad.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙkBabylon is suddenly fallen and destroyed: howl for her; take balm for her pain, if so be she may be healed.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse	¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙºWe would have healed Babylon, but she is not healed: forsake her, and let us go every one into his own country: for her judgment reacheth unto heaven, and is lifted up even to the skies.¦layout ªlast_verse	„¥verse
¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙmThe LORD hath brought forth our righteousness: come, and let us declare in Zion the work of the LORD our God.¦layout ªlast_verse
…¤book§chapter3«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙáMake bright the arrows; gather the shields: the LORD hath raised up the spirit of the kings of the Medes: for his device is against Babylon, to destroy it; because it is the vengeance of the LORD, the vengeance of his temple.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙĞSet up the standard upon the walls of Babylon, make the watch strong, set up the watchmen, prepare the ambushes: for the LORD hath both devised and done that which he spake against the inhabitants of Babylon.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙuO thou that dwellest upon many waters, abundant in treasures, thine end is come, and the measure of thy covetousness.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ•The LORD of hosts hath sworn by himself, saying, Surely I will fill thee with men, as with caterpillers; and they shall lift up a shout against thee.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter3«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‰He hath made the earth by his power, he hath established the world by his wisdom, and hath stretched out the heaven by his understanding.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙİWhen he uttereth his voice, there is a multitude of waters in the heavens; and he causeth the vapours to ascend from the ends of the earth: he maketh lightnings with rain, and bringeth forth the wind out of his treasures.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙšEvery man is brutish by his knowledge; every founder is confounded by the graven image: for his molten image is falsehood, and there is no breath in them.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙWThey are vanity, the work of errors: in the time of their visitation they shall perish.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ“The portion of Jacob is not like them; for he is the former of all things: and Israel is the rod of his inheritance: the LORD of hosts is his name.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter3«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses—„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙƒThou art my battle axe and weapons of war: for with thee will I break in pieces the nations, and with thee will I destroy kingdoms;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ}and with thee will I break in pieces the horse and his rider; and with thee will I break in pieces the chariot and his rider;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ©with thee also will I break in pieces man and woman; and with thee will I break in pieces old and young; and with thee will I break in pieces the young man and the maid;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÅI will also break in pieces with thee the shepherd and his flock; and with thee will I break in pieces the husbandman and his yoke of oxen; and with thee will I break in pieces captains and rulers.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd I will render unto Babylon and to all the inhabitants of Chaldea all their evil that they have done in Zion in your sight, saith the LORD.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÕBehold, I am against thee, O destroying mountain, saith the LORD, which destroyest all the earth: and I will stretch out mine hand upon thee, and roll thee down from the rocks, and will make thee a burnt mountain.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‡And they shall not take of thee a stone for a corner, nor a stone for foundations; but thou shalt be desolate for ever, saith the LORD.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter3«first_verseªlast_verse!¦verses—„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÚSet ye up a standard in the land, blow the trumpet among the nations, prepare the nations against her, call together against her the kingdoms of Ararat, Minni, and Ashchenaz; appoint a captain against her; cause the horses to come up as the rough caterpillers.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙPrepare against her the nations with the kings of the Medes, the captains thereof, and all the rulers thereof, and all the land of his dominion.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¨And the land shall tremble and sorrow: for every purpose of the LORD shall be performed against Babylon, to make the land of Babylon a desolation without an inhabitant.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¼The mighty men of Babylon have forborn to fight, they have remained in their holds: their might hath failed; they became as women: they have burned her dwellingplaces; her bars are broken.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ…One post shall run to meet another, and one messenger to meet another, to shew the king of Babylon that his city is taken at one end,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙoand that the passages are stopped, and the reeds they have burned with fire, and the men of war are affrighted.¦layout ªlast_verse „¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¼For thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; The daughter of Babylon is like a threshingfloor, it is time to thresh her: yet a little while, and the time of her harvest shall come.¦layout ªlast_verse!…¤book§chapter3«first_verse"ªlast_verse(¦verses—„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÑNebuchadrezzar the king of Babylon hath devoured me, he hath crushed me, he hath made me an empty vessel, he hath swallowed me up like a dragon, he hath filled his belly with my delicates, he hath cast me out.¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙThe violence done to me and to my flesh be upon Babylon, shall the inhabitant of Zion say; and my blood upon the inhabitants of Chaldea, shall Jerusalem say.¦layout ªlast_verse#„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙTherefore thus saith the LORD; Behold, I will plead thy cause, and take vengeance for thee; and I will dry up her sea, and make her springs dry.¦layout ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙtAnd Babylon shall become heaps, a dwellingplace for dragons, an astonishment, and an hissing, without an inhabitant.¦layout ªlast_verse%„¥verse&¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFThey shall roar together like lions: they shall yell as lions' whelps.¦layout ªlast_verse&„¥verse'¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ—In their heat I will make their feasts, and I will make them drunken, that they may rejoice, and sleep a perpetual sleep, and not wake, saith the LORD.¦layout ªlast_verse'„¥verse(¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙLI will bring them down like lambs to the slaughter, like rams with he goats.¦layout ªlast_verse(…¤book§chapter3«first_verse)ªlast_verse,¦verses”„¥verse)¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙƒHow is Sheshach taken! and how is the praise of the whole earth surprised! how is Babylon become an astonishment among the nations!¦layout ªlast_verse)„¥verse*¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙXThe sea is come up upon Babylon: she is covered with the multitude of the waves thereof.¦layout ªlast_verse*„¥verse+¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ„Her cities are a desolation, a dry land, and a wilderness, a land wherein no man dwelleth, neither doth any son of man pass thereby.¦layout ªlast_verse+„¥verse,¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÊAnd I will punish Bel in Babylon, and I will bring forth out of his mouth that which he hath swallowed up: and the nations shall not flow together any more unto him: yea, the wall of Babylon shall fall.¦layout ªlast_verse,…¤book§chapter3«first_verse-ªlast_verse2¦verses–„¥verse-¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙnMy people, go ye out of the midst of her, and deliver ye every man his soul from the fierce anger of the LORD.¦layout ªlast_verse-„¥verse.¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÜAnd lest your heart faint, and ye fear for the rumour that shall be heard in the land; a rumour shall both come one year, and after that in another year shall come a rumour, and violence in the land, ruler against ruler.¦layout ªlast_verse.„¥verse/¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¶Therefore, behold, the days come, that I will do judgment upon the graven images of Babylon: and her whole land shall be confounded, and all her slain shall fall in the midst of her.¦layout ªlast_verse/„¥verse0¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ”Then the heaven and the earth, and all that is therein, shall sing for Babylon: for the spoilers shall come unto her from the north, saith the LORD.¦layout ªlast_verse0„¥verse1¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙhAs Babylon hath caused the slain of Israel to fall, so at Babylon shall fall the slain of all the earth.¦layout ªlast_verse1„¥verse2¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ|Ye that have escaped the sword, go away, stand not still: remember the LORD afar off, and let Jerusalem come into your mind.¦layout ªlast_verse2…¤book§chapter3«first_verse3ªlast_verse:¦verses˜„¥verse3¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‘We are confounded, because we have heard reproach: shame hath covered our faces: for strangers are come into the sanctuaries of the LORD's house.¦layout ªlast_verse3„¥verse4¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ“Wherefore, behold, the days come, saith the LORD, that I will do judgment upon her graven images: and through all her land the wounded shall groan.¦layout ªlast_verse4„¥verse5¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙThough Babylon should mount up to heaven, and though she should fortify the height of her strength, yet from me shall spoilers come unto her, saith the LORD.¦layout ªlast_verse5„¥verse6¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ[A sound of a cry cometh from Babylon, and great destruction from the land of the Chaldeans:¦layout ªlast_verse6„¥verse7¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙbecause the LORD hath spoiled Babylon, and destroyed out of her the great voice; when her waves do roar like great waters, a noise of their voice is uttered:¦layout ªlast_verse7„¥verse8¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¯because the spoiler is come upon her, even upon Babylon, and her mighty men are taken, every one of their bows is broken: for the LORD God of recompences shall surely requite.¦layout ªlast_verse8„¥verse9¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÍAnd I will make drunk her princes, and her wise men, her captains, and her rulers, and her mighty men: and they shall sleep a perpetual sleep, and not wake, saith the King, whose name is The LORD of hosts.¦layout ªlast_verse9„¥verse:¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ×Thus saith the LORD of hosts; The broad walls of Babylon shall be utterly broken, and her high gates shall be burned with fire; and the people shall labour in vain, and the folk in the fire, and they shall be weary.¦layout ªlast_verse:…¤book§chapter3«first_verse;ªlast_verse@¦verses–„¥verse;¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙİThe word which Jeremiah the prophet commanded Seraiah the son of Neriah, the son of Maaseiah, when he went with Zedekiah the king of Judah into Babylon in the fourth year of his reign. And this Seraiah was a quiet prince.¦layout ªlast_verse;„¥verse<¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ~So Jeremiah wrote in a book all the evil that should come upon Babylon, even all these words that are written against Babylon.¦layout ªlast_verse<„¥verse=¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙiAnd Jeremiah said to Seraiah, When thou comest to Babylon, and shalt see, and shalt read all these words;¦layout ªlast_verse=„¥verse>¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ­then shalt thou say, O LORD, thou hast spoken against this place, to cut it off, that none shall remain in it, neither man nor beast, but that it shall be desolate for ever.¦layout ªlast_verse>„¥verse?¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd it shall be, when thou hast made an end of reading this book, that thou shalt bind a stone to it, and cast it into the midst of Euphrates:¦layout ªlast_verse?„¥verse@¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines’‚¤textÙ‚and thou shalt say, Thus shall Babylon sink, and shall not rise from the evil that I will bring upon her: and they shall be weary.¦layout ‚¤textÙ#Thus far are the words of Jeremiah.¦layout©paragraphªlast_verse@